WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.800
Casting on if you are a hand knitter,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.320
you probably know that there are
lots of different cast-on methods.

3
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.560
The same is true for machine knitters.

4
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.280
Even in crochet, you often work
with a foundation row to get started.

5
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.120
There are two basic cast-on types
open cast-on's and closed cast-on's.

6
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.440
Open is usually a very quick
way of getting started.

7
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:32.600
A provisional type cast on is another
somewhat open cast on where you can pick

8
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.720
up stitches and finish
knitting at a later time.

9
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.520
Maybe you'll start your garment

10
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.280
on the machine with an open cast
on and then pick up the stitches on hand

11
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.280
knitting needles and create
a pretty ruffle or ribbing by hand.

12
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:53.240
There are lots of reasons to use an open
on. But more often we don't want live

13
00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:00.040
stitches, we want a permanent, closed
finished edge that will be attractive on its own.

14
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.000
Part of the decision on which cast-on method to use is the purpose of your

15
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.440
edge. Will this edge be
the finished edge of your garment?

16
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.520
Will you be picking up stitches
and maybe working a trim?

17
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.800
Will you be crocheting around
the edge of your item?

18
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.160
Will this edge be hidden
by something else?

19
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.000
There's a lot to consider.

20
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.480
Here's the open cast-on that many

21
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.120
beginners are taught. It's good
for casting out a few stitches quickly.

22
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.640
Make sure your carriage is set to neutral,
it's set for plain knitting.

23
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.160
Thread the tensioning
system in the carriage.

24
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.840
Depending on your yarn,

25
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.480
said the carriage tension to somewhere
around the middle of the dial.

26
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.920
Put 20 to 30 stitches into working

27
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.360
position.
Holding the tail of the yarn below

28
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.720
the carriage with one hand push
the carriage across the needles from right

29
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.400
to left, your needles
should look like this.

30
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.680
If your tension is too loose,

31
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.920
the loops are going to be loose and sloppy
and they may not form stitches well.

32
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.760
Too tight of a tension will
cause problems as well.

33
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.600
Now, place your ravel cord across
the stitches between the needle hooks

34
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.040
and the gate pegs. You don't want the cord
in the hooks just next to the hooks.

35
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:35.040
Pull down on the ends of the ravel
cord and hold the yarn tail as well.

36
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.600
You'll want the cord to be below

37
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:45.400
the needles holding those loops in place
now, knit 4 or 5 rows.

38
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.360
Remove the ravel cord.

39
00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.560
You'll see now why ravel cord
needs to be strong and slippery.

40
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.560
Continue knitting.

41
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.800
Here are the live stitches
that are left behind.

42
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.040
Now, another negative for this particular

43
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.240
method is that it's difficult to use
over a large number of stitches.

44
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.840
It's important that you put pressure

45
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.240
on the main yarn loops with the ravel
cord. So if you have a lot of stitches,

46
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.600
it's hard to get that pressure
in the middle of the piece.

47
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.120
So the stitches knit properly.

48
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.040
Let's try a different method.

49
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.160
Many knitting machines come
with a cast-on comb.

50
00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.960
This allows you to quickly work an open

51
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.760
cast on over as many stitches
as you'd like. As before,

52
00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.840
Make sure your carriage is set to neutral.

53
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.760
The tension is set according to your yarn.

54
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.360
Put 20 or 30 needles into work, thread

55
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:07.600
up your machine and knit across
from right to left.

56
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.400
Next, hang the teeth of your cast-on comb

57
00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.400
in the loops of the yarn
from front to back.

58
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.640
Be sure to catch all the loops
so each needle knits properly.

59
00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.120
Knit back from left to right.

60
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.840
Like the ravel cord,

61
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:33.280
the cast on puts pressure on those
loops so the stitches will knit.

62
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.480
Did all your stitches knit?

63
00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.720
Here's a problem,
if you look closely,

64
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.000
my cast on Combe has some bent teeth,
and I didn't catch each loop.

65
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.880
Now that I'm knitting, you can see that
the stitches aren't forming correctly.

66
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.120
This cast on comb could be used for other

67
00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:56.240
techniques, but it's in really bad shape
and can't really be used for an open cast

68
00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:02.440
on even hanging the teeth in the other
direction from the back isn't helping.

69
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.520
There is no hope for this cast on comb.

70
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.280
As you can probably tell,
I'm not a fan of open cast on methods.

71
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.640
Let's move on to closed cast-ons.

72
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.120
They can be plain or fancy.
Really fast or somewhat tedious.

73
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.440
Close cast on methods are what
you will use most of the time.

